"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt;Jul 14, 2020&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/td&gt;&lt;td colspan="3" style="padding:2px 1.02pt 2px 1pt;text-align:right;vertical-align:bottom"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt;Jul 14, 2030&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/td&gt;&lt;/tr&gt;&lt;tr&gt;&lt;td colspan="3" style="padding:2px 1pt;text-align:left;vertical-align:bottom"&gt;&lt;div style="margin-bottom:1.5pt;margin-top:3.75pt;padding-left:0.02pt;padding-right:0.02pt"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:100%"&gt;4.93% Notes&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:6.5pt;font-weight:400;line-height:100%;position:relative;top:-3.5pt;vertical-align:baseline"&gt;(1)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;/td&gt;&lt;td colspan="3" style="padding:2px 1pt;text-align:left;vertical-align:bottom"&gt;&lt;div style="margin-bottom:1.5pt;margin-top:3.75pt;padding-right:-1.88pt;text-align:right"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:100%"&gt;$&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;150,000&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;/td&gt;&lt;td colspan="3" style="padding:2px 1pt;text-align:left;vertical-align:bottom"&gt;&lt;div style="margin-bottom:1.5pt;margin-top:3.75pt;padding-right:-1.88pt;text-align:right"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:100%"&gt;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;4.93&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;%&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;/td&gt;&lt;td colspan="3" style="padding:2px 1.02pt 2px 1pt;text-align:right;vertical-align:bottom"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt;May 28 and Nov 28&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/td&gt;&lt;td colspan="3" style="padding:2px 1.02pt 2px 1pt;text-align:right;vertical-align:bottom"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt;Mar 17, 2026&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/td&gt;&lt;td colspan="3" style="padding:2px 1.02pt 2px 1pt;text-align:right;vertical-align:bottom"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt;May 28, 2031&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/td&gt;&lt;/tr&gt;&lt;tr&gt;&lt;td colspan="3" style="padding:2px 1pt;text-align:left;vertical-align:bottom"&gt;&lt;div style="margin-bottom:1.5pt;margin-top:3.75pt;padding-left:0.02pt;padding-right:0.02pt"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:100%"&gt;5.68% Notes&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:6.5pt;font-weight:400;line-height:100%;position:relative;top:-3.5pt;vertical-align:baseline"&gt;(2)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;/td&gt;&lt;td colspan="3" style="padding:2px 1pt;text-align:left;vertical-align:bottom"&gt;&lt;div style="margin-bottom:1.5pt;margin-top:3.75pt;padding-right:-1.88pt;text-align:right"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:100%"&gt;$&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;125,000&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;/td&gt;&lt;td colspan="3" style="padding:2px 1pt;text-align:left;vertical-align:bottom"&gt;&lt;div style="margin-bottom:1.5pt;margin-top:3.75pt;padding-right:-1.88pt;text-align:right"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:100%"&gt;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;5.68&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;%&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;/td&gt;&lt;td colspan="3" style="padding:2px 1.02pt 2px 1pt;text-align:right;vertical-align:bottom"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt;Jun 18 and Dec 18&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/td&gt;&lt;td colspan="3" style="padding:2px 1.02pt 2px 1pt;text-align:right;vertical-align:bottom"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt;Nov 6, 2024&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/td&gt;&lt;td colspan="3" style="padding:2px 1.02pt 2px 1pt;text-align:right;vertical-align:bottom"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt;Dec 18, 2034&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/td&gt;&lt;/tr&gt;&lt;/table&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="margin-top:3pt;padding-left:45pt;text-indent:-27pt"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:8pt;font-weight:400;line-height:139%"&gt;(1)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:8pt;font-weight:400;line-height:139%;padding-left:17.24pt"&gt;The issuance was delayed until May 28, 2026 and the proceeds will be used to pay off the Respondent&#8217;s 4.66% Notes.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="padding-left:45pt;text-indent:-27pt"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:8pt;font-weight:400;line-height:139%"&gt;(2)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:8pt;font-weight:400;line-height:139%;padding-left:17.24pt"&gt;The issuance was delayed until December 18, 2024.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="padding-left:9pt;text-indent:-9pt"&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;br/&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="margin-top:6pt"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-style:italic;font-weight:700;line-height:139%"&gt;Debt Covenants&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="margin-top:6pt;text-indent:27pt"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:139%"&gt;The Respondent&#8217;s 4.66% Notes, 3.79% Notes, 5.68% Notes, and its 4.93% Notes each contain negative covenants that, subject to significant exceptions, limit the ability of the Respondent to, among other things, (i) incur debt, (ii) incur liens, (iii) engage in mergers, consolidations, liquidations and dissolutions, (iv) dispose of substantially all of the assets of the Respondent and its subsidiaries, (v) make certain restricted payments and (vi) enter into transactions with affiliates. The covenants require the Respondent to comply on a quarterly basis with a capitalization ratio. If an event of default occurs, the commitments of the Lenders to lend under the facility may be terminated and the maturity of the amounts outstanding may be accelerated.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="margin-top:6pt"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:700;line-height:139%"&gt;4. COMMITMENTS AND CONTINGENCIES&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="margin-top:6pt"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-style:italic;font-weight:700;line-height:139%"&gt;Regulatory and Rate Matters and Related Litigation&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="margin-top:6pt"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-style:italic;font-weight:400;line-height:139%"&gt;Fuel Filing&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="margin-bottom:6pt;margin-top:6pt;text-indent:27pt"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:139%"&gt;The Respondent recovers the natural gas it uses for fuel on its operating system and gas losses it incurs on its system in-kind from its customers via a fuel reimbursement charge placed on the volumes of gas transported through the system. The reimbursement charge is established through an annual fuel tracker filed with the FERC.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="margin-top:9pt"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-style:italic;font-weight:700;line-height:139%"&gt;Environmental and Safety Matters&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="margin-top:6pt"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-style:italic;font-weight:400;line-height:139%"&gt;Environmental &lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="margin-top:6pt;text-indent:27pt"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:139%"&gt;The Respondent is subject to federal, state and local statutes, rules and regulations relating to environmental protection, including the National Environmental Policy Act, the Clean Water Act, the Clean Air Act, the Toxic Substances Control Act, and the Resource Conservation and Recovery Act. Compliance with these laws and regulations can result in capital, operating and other costs, which generally subject the Respondent to inspections and require it to obtain and comply with a wide variety of environmental licenses, permits and other approvals. In addition, the Respondent is subject to requirements to report, investigate and clean up releases of pollutants found on or under properties that the Respondent owns or operates and any impacted neighboring properties. Many of the environmental programs are subject to frequent changes, based upon new legislation, new regulations, court challenges to regulations and changes in conditions affecting the Respondent&#8217;s operations.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="margin-top:6pt;text-indent:27pt"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:139%"&gt;The Respondent has identified polychlorinated biphenyl (PCB) contamination in air compressor systems, soils, and related properties at certain compressor station sites. All of the known areas of PCB contamination at compressor stations have been remediated except for two compressor station sites where PCB is encapsulated in concrete. The removal and remediation of PCBs at these two compressor stations will likely occur when the stations are decommissioned. The Respondent has accrued an undiscounted liability of approximately $0.6 million as of March&#160;31, 2026 and December 31, 2025, representing the current estimate of future environmental &lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="margin-top:6pt"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:139%"&gt;testing and cleanup costs (relating to known and suspected contamination by PCBs). The Respondent considers environmental assessment, remediation, and costs associated with compliance with environmental standards to be recoverable through rates, as they are prudent costs incurred in the ordinary course of business. The costs and timing of expenditures incurred will depend on the actual amount and extent of contamination discovered, the final cleanup standards mandated by the U.S. Environmental Protection Agency (EPA) or other governmental authorities, and the duration of related negotiations with governmental authorities, among other factors.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="margin-top:6pt;text-indent:27pt"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:139%"&gt;Greenhouse gases (GHG) including methane and carbon dioxide, are currently federally regulated air pollutants. New or modified sources of GHG may require obtaining operating permits and implementation of controls to reduce GHG emissions. &lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="margin-top:6pt;text-indent:27pt"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:139%"&gt;In June 2016, the EPA expanded its 2012 New Source Performance Standards for the oil and gas industry, NSPS OOOOa, to achieve both methane reductions and additional reductions in volatile organic compounds. The rule&#8217;s focus is specific to the oil and natural gas sectors, setting emission standards for methane and adding requirements to previously unaffected sources. The rule requires reductions in emissions from the Respondent&#8217;s operations and imposes pollution control and other requirements that affect the Respondent&#8217;s ability to build, replace or modify equipment. The standards have been under continuous judicial review. In November 2021, the EPA proposed changes to the existing rule OOOOa, and proposed two new subparts to the rule, one for new sources going forward, OOOOb, and one for existing sources, OOOOc. At this time, the final rule is being litigated throughout the Courts, and EPA has taken the regulation under reconsideration. Court decisions and/or EPA updates are expected in Q2/Q3 2026. &lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="margin-top:6pt;text-indent:27pt"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:139%"&gt;In February 2022, the EPA proposed a Federal Implementation Plan to control NOx from sources in 26 states in an effort to prevent non-attainment with the 2015 ozone standard in downwind states. The &#8220;Good Neighbor Rule&#8221; requires retrofits of engines over 1,000 hp in Oklahoma, Missouri and Wyoming. Until such changes are finalized, it is not possible to assess their impacts or predict the impacts on the Respondent's business, financial position and operations. This rule has been stayed by the Supreme Court and is awaiting decision from the D.C. Circuit for a path forward. Their decision is expected sometime in 2026. This rule has also been addressed via reconsideration by the Trump Administration and appears to be permanently stayed at this time. &lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="margin-top:6pt;text-indent:27pt"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:139%"&gt;In June 2022, the EPA published updates to the Mandatory Reporting Rule for GHG. Changes include higher emission factors for fugitive leaks and combustion emissions. This is currently under review by the EPA. The EPA is in the midst of significant deregulation and it is unclear at this time how this rule will be impacted. &lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="margin-top:6pt;text-indent:27pt"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:139%"&gt;All of the Respondent&#8217;s facilities are located in areas currently designated as being in &#8220;attainment&#8221; of all National Ambient Air Quality Standards for all criteria air pollutants. These areas are subject to monitoring and periodic updates to their attainment classifications. Revised ambient air quality standards could result in certain areas being classified as non-attainment that were formerly in attainment status. If a non-attainment designation occurs in areas in which the Respondent operates, it could ultimately result in changes to the air pollution control requirements to which the Respondent is subject, and increase both its capital and operating costs.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="margin-top:6pt;text-indent:27pt"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:139%"&gt;Environmental laws and regulations are subject to frequent revisions through new legislation on the state or federal level, new regulations and court challenges and related decisions. The final results of any pending proposals and court cases, as well as future proposed legislative or regulatory changes, are generally difficult to predict until such changes are adopted in final legislation, regulations or court decisions. Until such changes are finalized, it is not possible to assess their impacts or predict the impacts on the Respondent&#8217;s business, financial position and operations.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="margin-top:6pt"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-style:italic;font-weight:700;line-height:139%"&gt;Summary of Commitments and Contingencies&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="margin-top:6pt;text-indent:27pt"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:139%"&gt;The Respondent is a party to various legal, regulatory and other matters arising from the day-to-day operations of our businesses that may result in claims against the Respondent. Although no assurance can be given, the Respondent believes, based on our experiences to date, that the ultimate resolution of such items will &lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="margin-top:6pt"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:139%"&gt;not have a material adverse impact to its business. The Respondent believes it has meritorious defenses to the matters to which it is a party and intends to vigorously defend itself. When the Respondent determines a loss is probable of occurring and is reasonably estimable, it accrues an undiscounted liability for such contingencies based on its best estimate using information available at that time. Because it presently is not possible to determine the outcome of these matters, no provision has been made in the financial statements for their ultimate resolution. In management&#8217;s opinion, the final resolution of all legal matters will not have a material adverse effect on the Respondent&#8217;s financial position. &lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="margin-bottom:6pt;margin-top:6pt"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:700;line-height:139%"&gt;5. INCOME TAXES&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="text-indent:27pt"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:133%"&gt;The Respondent&#8217;s effective tax rates for the three-month periods ended March 31, 2026 and 2025 were 38.3% and 23.8%, respectively. Generally, the Respondent&#8217;s effective tax rate in excess of or less than the federal statutory rate is primarily due to the impact of state income taxes, including state income tax incentives. The increase in the 2026 effective tax rate is primarily attributable to the remeasurement of deferred tax assets and liabilities associated with Kansas income taxes. The remeasurement reflects the expected impact of Kansas&#8217; transition from a three-factor apportionment methodology to a single sales factor apportionment for tax years beginning in 2027. &lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="margin-bottom:6pt;margin-top:6pt;text-indent:27pt"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:139%"&gt;As of March&#160;31, 2026, the Respondent remained subject to examination for the tax year 2022 and forward for federal jurisdictions and 2021 and forward for state jurisdictions.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="margin-top:6pt"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:700;line-height:139%"&gt;6. EMPLOYEE BENEFIT PLANS &lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="margin-top:6pt;text-indent:27pt"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:139%"&gt;The Respondent maintains two pension plans, the Union Pension Plan and the Non-Union Pension Plan, and a postretirement benefit other than pension (PBOP) plan. The PBOP plan is comprised of the Non-Union voluntary employees&#8217; beneficiary association (VEBA) trust and the Union VEBA trust.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="margin-top:6pt;text-indent:27pt"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:139%"&gt;The Union and Non-Union Pension Plans are provided to certain employees who retire under the Respondent&#8217;s pension retirement plans. The pension plans are noncontributory defined benefit plans. Eligible employees under these plans are those who were hired prior to June 1, 2017, who qualify for retirement benefits under these plans, and who meet certain service and other requirements.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="margin-bottom:6pt;margin-top:6pt;text-indent:27pt"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:139%"&gt;The PBOP plan provides postretirement medical and life insurance benefits to certain employees who retire under the Respondent&#8217;s PBOP retirement plan. The PBOP plan is contributory for medical and, for some retired employees, contributory for life insurance benefits in excess of specified limits. Eligible employees under this plan are those hired prior to various qualifying dates, the latest of which is December 31, 1995, who qualify for retirement benefits under this plan, and who meet certain service and other requirements.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="text-indent:27pt"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:133%"&gt;The terms of the RP21-778 rate settlement allow the Respondent to recover, in its rates, approximately $9.6 million annually for pension benefits and postretirement benefits other than pensions. The Respondent must fund the amounts recovered into irrevocable trusts established solely for the provision of the aforementioned benefits in a manner that permits the Respondent to maximize the tax deductibility of the deposits and adhere to minimum and maximum funding requirements. The Respondent&#8217;s $9.6 million annual funding requirement may only be reduced by amounts funded in excess of recoveries in prior years. As of December 31, 2025, the Respondent&#8217;s funding was equivalent to its RP21-778 recoveries.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="margin-top:6pt;text-indent:27pt"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:139%"&gt;The Compensation - Retirement Benefits Topic 715 of the ASC (ASC 715) requires companies to recognize the funded status of their defined benefit pension and other postretirement benefit plans as a net liability or asset in their balance sheets and to recognize changes in that funded status in the year in which changes occur through comprehensive income. As it is appropriate for the Respondent to apply the accounting prescribed by Regulated Operations Topic 980 of the ASC (ASC 980) the Respondent does not recognize changes in the funded status in comprehensive income but recognizes them as changes to the related regulatory asset or liability, pending future recovery or refund through its rates. &lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="margin-top:6pt;text-indent:27pt"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:139%"&gt;Pursuant to ASC 715, no portion of the related liabilities are classified as current because plan assets exceed the value of benefit obligations expected to be paid within the 12 months ending March 31, 2027. In addition, no plan assets are expected to be returned to the Respondent during the 12 months ending March 31, 2027. &lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="margin-top:6pt"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:700;line-height:139%"&gt;7. RELATED PARTY TRANSACTIONS &lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="margin-top:6pt;text-indent:27pt"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:161%"&gt;The Respondent is a party to a Federal and State Income Tax Policy with Southern Star.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="margin-top:6pt;text-indent:27pt"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:161%"&gt;During the first three months of 2026, the Respondent paid dividends of approximately $26.5 million to Southern Star.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="margin-top:6pt;text-indent:27pt"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:161%"&gt;The Respondent utilizes a cash management program with Southern Star, whereby cash may be advanced to or from the Respondent, to be repaid at any time as needed to fund operations. The advances earn interest based on the daily advance balance computed at the three-month U.S. Treasury rate as stated on the first day of each month. The Respondent had an advance to Southern Star for $53.5 million and $19.1 million at March&#160;31, 2026 and December 31, 2025, respectively.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="margin-top:6pt"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:700;line-height:139%"&gt;8. NEW ACCOUNTING PRONOUNCEMENTS &lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="margin-top:6pt;text-indent:27pt"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:139%"&gt;On December 14, 2023, the FASB issued ASU 2023-09, &#8220;Income Taxes (Topic 740).&#8221; The amendments in this ASU require qualitative disclosure about specific categories of reconciling items and individual jurisdictions that result in a significant difference between the statutory rate and the effective tax rate. Disclosures will be required for income taxes paid (net of refunds), broken out between federal, state/local and foreign, and amounts paid to an individual jurisdiction when 5.0% or more of the total income taxes paid. The amendments in this ASU are effective for the Respondent&#8217;s annual reporting periods beginning after December 15, 2025. Management adopted this ASU as of January 1, 2026.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="margin-top:6pt;text-indent:27pt"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:139%"&gt;On July 30, 2025, the FASB issued ASU 2025-05, &#8220;Financial Instruments&#8211;Credit Losses (Topic 326).&#8221; The amendments in this ASU provide all entities with a practical expedient and entities other than public business entities with an accounting policy election when estimating expected credit losses for current accounts receivable and current contract assets arising from transactions accounted for under Topic 606. The amendments in this ASU are effective for the Respondent&#8217;s annual reporting periods beginning after December 15, 2025. Management adopted this ASU as of January 1, 2026. Management does not believe this ASU will have a material impact on the financial statements of the Respondent.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="margin-top:6pt;text-indent:27pt"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:139%"&gt;On September 18, 2025, the FASB issued ASU 2025-06, &#8220;Intangibles&#8211;Goodwill and Other&#8211;Internal-Use Software (Subtopic 350-40).&#8221; The amendments in this ASU remove all references to prescriptive and sequential software development stages throughout Subtopic 350-40. Therefore, an entity is required to start capitalizing software costs when both of the following occur: 1) management has authorized and committed to funding the software project and 2) it is probable that the project will be completed and the software will be used to perform the function intended. The amendments in this ASU will be effective for the Respondent&#8217;s annual reporting periods beginning after December 15, 2027. Management is currently assessing the impact this ASU will have on the financial statements of the Respondent.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="margin-top:6pt;text-indent:27pt"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:139%"&gt;On December 8, 2025, the FASB issued ASU 2025-11, &#8220;Interim Reporting (Topic 270).&#8221; The amendments in this ASU clarify interim disclosure requirements and provide additional guidance on what disclosures should be provided in interim reporting periods. The amendments in this ASU will be effective for the Respondent&#8217;s annual reporting periods beginning after December 15, 2028. Management is currently assessing the impact this ASU will have on the financial statements of the Respondent.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="margin-top:6pt;text-indent:27pt"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:139%"&gt;On December 17, 2025, the FASB issued ASU 2025-12, &#8220;Codification Improvements.&#8221; The amendments in this ASU include 33 codification changes that clarify, correct errors, or make minor improvements across a variety &lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="margin-top:6pt"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:139%"&gt;of topics. The amendments in this ASU will be effective for the Respondent&#8217;s annual reporting periods beginning after December 15, 2026. Management is currently assessing the impact this ASU will have on the financial statements of the Respondent.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="margin-top:6pt"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:700;line-height:139%"&gt;9. SUBSEQUENT EVENTS &lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="margin-bottom:6pt;margin-top:6pt;text-indent:27pt"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:139%"&gt;Management has evaluated all events or transactions that occurred from March&#160;31, 2026 through May&#160;20, 2026, the date these financial statements were available to be issued. &lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="margin-bottom:6pt;margin-top:6pt;text-indent:27pt"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:139%"&gt;There were no subsequent events that required adjustment to the financial statements or disclosures.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;</ferc:DisclosureOfImportantDisclosuresRegardingTheFinancialStatementsTextBlock>
